00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:20.750
Okay, so I'm gonna talk about kind of

00:08:20.865 --> 00:08:24.000
the brief history of circuit QED

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.860
and then talk about some new results

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:33.850
that we've been working on with

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:38.130
our many collaborators here in YQI.

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:42.630
So I want to give a
special thanks to the ARO

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:44.850
for their strong support

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:49.200
of this burgeoning field over
the last quarter century.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:53.190
And it's been very important
to the history that I'm gonna

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:54.840
tell you about now.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:56.850
And a lot of, you know,

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:59.790
the senior people will
know the history and,

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:02.260
but some of the younger
people don't know all of

00:09:03.287 --> 00:09:05.460
the things shrouded in the midst of time.

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:09.570
So I thought it'd be fun
to go over that a bit.

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:13.140
And the field started with

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:14.550
classical mechanics

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:19.550
and the realization that the
Josephson equations told us

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:24.360
that the phase across a
superconducting junction

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:28.521
obeyed Newton's classical law of motion.

00:09:28.521 --> 00:09:33.521
You could think of the phase
as the position of a particle

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:38.460
and it sits in a washboard potential.

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:40.800
If you have a current bias junction

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:42.870
and if it escapes from one of the wells,

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:45.270
it begins falling downhill.

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:50.220
And because the velocity

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:53.440
of the particle, the time
rate of change of the phase

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:59.130
is the voltage, you can
detect that velocity.

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:03.120
And for you young people who
don't know what a washboard is,

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:06.460
Mike Tinkham always used to say it's a

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:10.119
classical analog of a Joseph injunction.

00:10:10.119 --> 00:10:13.882
(audience laughing)

00:10:13.882 --> 00:10:16.590
So people started building

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:17.730
these circuits

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:20.370
and doing interesting things with 'em

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:23.160
in a world dominated by friction.

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.470
And Tony Leggett began asking

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:28.598
questions about Schrödinger cats and,

00:10:28.598 --> 00:10:30.820
and you know, if this particle, if this

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:35.010
variable acts like the
position of a particle, could

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:39.093
that particle exhibit
macroscopic quantum behavior.

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:44.430
And so our heroes, John Clarke

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:47.160
and Michel Devoret and,

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:52.080
and John Martinez in
1985 decided to see if

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:56.250
that particle had quantized energy levels

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:59.760
inside these little potential wells.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:03.960
And they did that by spectroscopy,

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:07.920
by kind of quantum jump spectroscopy,

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:12.180
where you shine microwaves at
the right transition frequency

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:15.510
and then the particle is
high enough up in the well

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:19.980
that it can tunnel out into
a finite voltage state.

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:22.380
So it's microwaves in

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:25.443
and low frequency
voltage measurements out.

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:30.900
And sure enough, they saw peaks
in the spectrum indicating

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:33.723
that this particle could
be quantum mechanical.

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:40.680
Then people started playing
with Cooper pair of boxes

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:44.290
and it's just a capacitor with

00:11:45.325 --> 00:11:46.803
a bias voltage on it.

00:11:47.974 --> 00:11:50.400
And it's a sufficiently small capacitor

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:53.290
that tuning this voltage could

00:11:54.992 --> 00:11:59.992
change the average number of
Cooper pairs from end to end,

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:03.030
plus one, from a billion
to a billion plus one.

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:05.640
And if you choose the
bias voltage correctly,

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:07.890
those two states can be degenerate.

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:10.890
And because of the coherent
Josephson tunneling,

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:14.100
you can go into a superposition
of those two charge states.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:17.850
And so unlike a normal junction,

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:22.850
the average charge would
change over a voltage range

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:26.700
that was controlled by the
coherent tunneling amplitude,

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:28.680
the coherent tunneling splitting

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:30.723
between those two charge states.

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:37.310
Also in Japan, Yasu
Nakamura was working on this

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:41.790
and he was doing microwave
measurements, again,

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:45.660
doing spectroscopy to see this splitting,

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:47.710
and then had a

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:54.300
destructive readout that
involved a complicated process in

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:56.970
which various voltages
were tuned in such a way

00:12:56.970 --> 00:12:58.650
that a Cooper pair could break

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:01.680
and tunnel out acquiring some energy

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:03.940
to break from the bias voltages

00:13:05.038 --> 00:13:08.790
and create a tiny tunneling
current in the circuit.

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:11.040
So again, microwaves in

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:14.250
and quite difficult,

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:17.673
noisy low frequency measurements
to see what happened.

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:20.100
But then in 1999,

00:13:23.554 --> 00:13:26.880
Yasu wrote the paper, which
got me into the field.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:30.000
I'd never heard of the
concept of quantum computing.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.820
I didn't know there were
crazy people thinking about

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:34.620
this possibility.

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:38.370
And he did this amazing
experiment where he

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.280
with a half a million dollar

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:44.550
picosecond pulse generator
moved the bias point

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:48.870
of the circuit to the
degeneracy point so fast

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:53.670
that the system was then
in a coherent superposition

00:13:53.670 --> 00:13:55.290
of the two eigenstates.

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:59.070
And it's the real time, you
know, it's the time domain

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:02.850
analog of Rabi oscillations
in a driven atom.

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:07.680
And here you can see these beautiful

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:08.880
Rabi oscillations,

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:11.470
but look at the timescale,
it's picoseconds

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:18.060
but still it was the
first time that this sort

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:21.060
of real time quantum
dynamics had been seen due

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:22.540
to this coherent splitting

00:14:25.356 --> 00:14:26.940
and this is a, you know, I don't have time

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:28.080
to do the whole history,

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:29.820
so I'm just picking out a few highlights.

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:33.840
So this is Quantronium
from the Saclay group

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:38.190
and it was an artificial atom tuned

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:41.250
to a clock transition, to a place

00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:45.990
where the splitting frequency
is first order insensitive

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:50.700
to the control knobs
that you can turn some

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:53.640
magnetic flux and some bias voltage.

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:56.130
And if you, you know,
a fundamental theorem

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:59.220
of engineering is that
if you can turn a knob,

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:01.860
the noise demon can also turn that knob.

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:04.920
So you want to be at a place

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:09.920
where you're first order is
insensitive to those effects.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.300
And this was, I remember Rob

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:16.560
and I discussing this thing
just seemed to ring forever,

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:18.360
you know, half a microsecond.

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:19.600
It was incredible

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:23.910
how long the coherence was.

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:26.790
And this was the first observation

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:29.790
of Ramsey fringes in a solid state qubit.

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:31.443
And why was this important?

00:15:32.699 --> 00:15:33.720
Because it's the thing we needed

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:36.900
to convince those pesky AMO physicists

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:39.840
that this was the real deal, okay?

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:44.163
This is their acid test for coherence.

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:47.130
So that was incredibly exciting.

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:48.750
and the experiment was done just

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:52.020
before Michelle moved to Yale

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:53.180
and really...

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:57.573
really moved things forward.

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:00.940
Hans Mooij in Delft

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:06.920
and also in some experiments
in collaboration with Yasu

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:12.240
developed a kind of dual to
these charge based qubits,

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:14.343
the flux qubit.

00:16:15.830 --> 00:16:19.770
And John Martinez developed a

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:21.420
high fidelity readout.

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:24.690
It was 85%, it was like amazing

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:27.090
high fidelity for those days.

00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:31.050
And it was going back
to his PhD thesis here.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:34.500
You had two levels that were
deep enough in the well,

00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:36.000
that they could be your qubit,

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:37.770
you could have a Rabi drive,

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:39.870
but then if you wanted to read it out,

00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:43.860
you could check whether it was
in this state by exciting it

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:46.380
to this state and then having a tunnel out

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:48.600
and release a flood of energy

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:50.730
as the particle fell down the hill.

00:16:50.730 --> 00:16:52.710
And that flood of energy was big enough

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:55.260
that he got this amazingly
high readout fidelity

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:57.480
for the time, 85%.

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:01.050
But unfortunately, when
you tried to do this

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:03.060
with two qubits, that amazing flood

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:05.926
of energy wiped out the other qubits.

00:17:05.926 --> 00:17:10.020
So in the end it was too destructive,

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:14.553
but it was a real advance in
terms of readout fidelity.

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:18.820
Then coming back to New Haven

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:23.100
with a couple people who
are here in the room,

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:27.724
the Transmon Qubit, sort of the
world's simplest thing, just

00:17:27.724 --> 00:17:29.400
a little dipole antenna

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:32.580
with the two halves connected
by a Joseph injunction.

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:36.240
It's not so little for an
atom, it's a millimeter across

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:40.800
the transition dipole
moment is several Cooper

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:42.120
pairs moving a millimeter,

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:45.603
which is a lot bigger than the
transition dipole moment of,

00:17:46.752 --> 00:17:47.902
of sodium, for example.

00:17:49.127 --> 00:17:51.600
And it had this wonderful feature

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:53.610
that it was exponentially insensitive

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:55.350
to offset charge noise.

00:17:55.350 --> 00:17:58.230
And you know, people

00:17:58.230 --> 00:18:00.840
that didn't experience those early days

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:03.210
won't understand why I had

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:06.460
PTSD looking at those old slides about

00:18:07.629 --> 00:18:10.170
that involved charge.

00:18:10.170 --> 00:18:15.170
So there's just stuff in the
substrate that moves around

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:17.010
and we can't fix that,

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:20.070
but we can make a qubit
that's insensitive to that.

00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:24.030
It's an artificial atom with
atomic number 10 to the 12,

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:27.180
and yet the spectrum is
simpler than that of hydrogen

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:29.913
and it has a comparable quality factor.

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:34.320
Nowadays many people are making

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:35.790
high fidelity gates.

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:38.550
Here's one example from
Will Oliver's group,

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:43.230
or two examples, a controlled
phase gate through a coupler

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:44.910
between two transmons

00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:49.910
and even higher fidelity gates between two

00:18:50.457 --> 00:18:54.240
flexonium qubits with a transmon coupler.

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.790
So it's just been tremendous progress.

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:59.040
You'll hear more from other
people during the week.

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:02.340
And in Rob's dual rail talk on Friday,

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:06.633
I think you'll also see some
amazing fidelity numbers.

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:12.190
And you know, a key part of the

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:17.940
the engineering is that you
need quantum limited amplifiers

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:20.620
to detect the very tiny signals that

00:19:22.830 --> 00:19:24.543
come out of these systems.

00:19:25.422 --> 00:19:27.840
And there are many people, many
of whom are in the audience

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:31.220
who contributed to this, Conrad is...

00:19:32.827 --> 00:19:36.240
is using them now to search
for dark matter axions

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:40.350
with our colleagues in the
Wright lab here at Yale.

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:41.760
And Michelle

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:46.440
and Zako used the very high
efficiency measurements to,

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:50.520
to catch and even reverse a
quantum jump in mid-flight.

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:54.693
So really very important
engineering developments.

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:59.703
Okay, so now onto really circuit QED.

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:04.780
So we know that QED is

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:10.020
really the study of the
effect of vacuum fluctuation.

00:20:10.020 --> 00:20:11.160
So the quantization

00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:14.640
of the electromagnetic field in the world.

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:16.860
And it has many effects

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:18.840
that you learn about not at the beginning

00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:20.220
of your quantum mechanics course,

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:22.050
but certainly in graduate school.

00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:25.650
It leads to spontaneous emission
of the first excito state

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:28.950
of hydrogen in 1.5 nanoseconds.

00:20:28.950 --> 00:20:32.560
It renormalizes the mass of
the electron, gives a splitting

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:37.050
between the lamb shift between the

00:20:37.050 --> 00:20:40.546
nominally degenerate states
of the hydrogen atom.

00:20:40.546 --> 00:20:44.340
And the engineering we're trying to do is

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:47.760
to change the spectrum of
the vacuum fluctuations

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:49.980
by putting them between mirrors

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:54.510
or even inside a superconducting
box if it's microwaves.

00:20:54.510 --> 00:20:57.217
And by changing that spectrum,

00:20:57.217 --> 00:20:59.760
you can engineer new properties

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:04.017
and in particular you can
have the Purcell effect to

00:21:04.017 --> 00:21:09.017
suppress the spontaneous
emission of the artificial atom

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:12.660
by not having any modes in the

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:15.960
cavity at that frequency.

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:20.190
That doesn't work in
macroscopic optical systems

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:21.900
because it can still radiate this way.

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:25.950
But inside a box, if you take
a Transmon in free space,

00:21:25.950 --> 00:21:28.080
it has a spontaneous emission lifetime

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:30.900
of a hundred nanoseconds,
can be a thousand times

00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:34.830
or more longer inside one of these boxes.

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:36.513
So that's very important.

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:42.630
So here's the original gang
with the first experiment,

00:21:42.630 --> 00:21:46.920
it's inside a shielded copper box

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:49.320
because we were sure that
all those cell phones

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:51.540
that were being invented in those days

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:53.550
were gonna screw up the experiment.

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:54.723
Turns out they didn't,

00:21:56.100 --> 00:22:00.720
but it was electromagnetically
quiet, so quiet inside there

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:03.007
that Dave Schuster
could sleep standing up.

00:22:03.007 --> 00:22:06.300
(audience laughing)

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:09.780
Okay, so...

00:22:09.780 --> 00:22:14.780
what we did in in in this
paper led by Alexandre Blais,

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:20.120
a theory paper was to get
rid of all the DC stuff

00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:25.110
and send in microwaves
in, get microwaves out.

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:27.690
And I didn't understand, you know,

00:22:27.690 --> 00:22:29.310
as a condensed matter physicist,

00:22:29.310 --> 00:22:30.900
I didn't know anything about quantum

00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:32.070
measurements in those days.

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:33.210
I had to learn this.

00:22:33.210 --> 00:22:36.240
And this really is sort of the

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:39.990
prototypical quantum measurement,
you send in the microwaves

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:41.550
they come back with a phase shift

00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:44.146
that depends on the
frequency of the cavity,

00:22:44.146 --> 00:22:46.770
which in turn depends on
the state of the qubit.

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:51.770
And so you literally entangle
the qubit with a meter,

00:22:51.900 --> 00:22:55.203
the photons, and then
you read out the meter.

00:22:56.482 --> 00:22:59.400
And I learned so much

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:02.670
from finally understanding
quantum measurements

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:03.990
from thinking about this.

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:05.793
It was a really wonderful time.

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:10.950
So Andreas

00:23:10.950 --> 00:23:15.950
and Dave managed to get
the vacuum Rabi splitting

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:17.550
for the first time in a circuit.

00:23:17.550 --> 00:23:19.800
And I remember when trying to calculate

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:22.860
how big was it gonna be and
you know, get the units right

00:23:22.860 --> 00:23:26.220
and everything, and it came
out a hundred megahertz

00:23:26.220 --> 00:23:29.010
and I thought, oh my gosh,
that's pretty gigantic.

00:23:29.010 --> 00:23:30.540
But then, you know, there was charge

00:23:30.540 --> 00:23:32.400
noise and it was horrible.

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:34.560
But they managed to...

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:37.140
to get at work sneaking in
at night when things were,

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:39.190
the elevator wasn't running or something.

00:23:40.048 --> 00:23:41.820
and that worked

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:45.870
and that illustrated strong
coupling that the coupling,

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:49.140
the dipole coupling was
bigger than the dissipation.

00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:50.580
Then Dave

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:55.230
and Jay went into the limit
where the second order effects

00:23:55.230 --> 00:23:59.100
of the coupling, the dispersive
coupling were still so big,

00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:00.930
they were bigger than the dissipation.

00:24:00.930 --> 00:24:04.440
And then you could count individual

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:06.240
microwave photons proving

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:09.130
that they really are particles not waves

00:24:11.160 --> 00:24:16.160
and also could see this
Purcell suppression.

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:20.220
Hanhee Paik did a 3D cavity experiment

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:22.950
and Matt Regor worked on making these

00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:25.470
cavities much longer lived.

00:24:25.470 --> 00:24:27.993
And now we have new designs and,

00:24:29.105 --> 00:24:31.320
and it's amazing now

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:35.673
the lifetimes you can
get out of these things.

00:24:37.388 --> 00:24:39.520
So the Santa Barbara group

00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:46.800
started making super positions
of small numbers of photons.

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:48.660
Here's the theoretical Wigner function

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:50.970
and the experimental
reconstructed Wigner function

00:24:50.970 --> 00:24:53.710
for zero plus one through zero plus five

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:56.850
from our friends here.

00:24:56.850 --> 00:25:00.600
And so we're, you know, and Rob

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:03.930
and company did an experiment
seeing the electric field

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:07.710
between the superposition
of zero and one photons

00:25:07.710 --> 00:25:10.320
and we were really into the world

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:13.410
of controlling individual quanta.

00:25:13.410 --> 00:25:15.760
And then this first

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:20.970
two qubit quantum processor
with the two qubits talking

00:25:20.970 --> 00:25:24.060
to each other through the
quantum bus of virtual exchange

00:25:24.060 --> 00:25:27.010
of microwave photons that was developed

00:25:27.010 --> 00:25:31.620
in Rob's lab by these guys.

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:35.140
And we were so excited to, you
know, run a quantum algorithm

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:37.140
with two qubits

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:40.743
and okay, it's a start, you
have to start somewhere.

00:25:42.012 --> 00:25:45.810
And I think Jerry will
tell us about some minor

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:47.757
scaling up that they've been doing

00:25:47.757 --> 00:25:49.388
(audience laughing)

00:25:49.388 --> 00:25:51.990
and in the last few years

00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:54.570
and we look forward to hearing that

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:56.460
is gonna be exciting.

00:25:56.460 --> 00:26:01.460
So here's the gang excited
about two qubit entanglement

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:06.213
and algorithms in 2007.

00:26:07.571 --> 00:26:10.312
And Dave doesn't look that excited,

00:26:10.312 --> 00:26:11.618
(audience laughing)

00:26:11.618 --> 00:26:13.080
but I think he was, I think he was happy.

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:13.913
I don't know.

00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:18.210
Okay, so I'm gonna,

00:26:18.210 --> 00:26:22.410
that's the past I want to
talk about more recent past

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:23.880
of quantum error correction

00:26:23.880 --> 00:26:27.320
and I'm just gonna briefly mention things

00:26:27.320 --> 00:26:31.440
with three different
experiments using bosonic codes

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:36.300
which have gotten to
slightly beyond break even

00:26:36.300 --> 00:26:39.120
and more recently substantially beyond.

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:42.060
And then I'll say a little bit more

00:26:42.060 --> 00:26:43.623
on the theory end later.

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:48.330
So the first code

00:26:48.330 --> 00:26:53.330
was the cat code that
was developed by our,

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:56.770
within collaboration
with theory collaborators

00:26:58.209 --> 00:27:02.020
and experiment in Rob's lab in 2016

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:08.100
getting slightly above
breakeven using Schrodinger

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:11.790
cat states with the either
super position of two positions

00:27:11.790 --> 00:27:13.083
or two momenta.

00:27:14.220 --> 00:27:18.900
And then Luyan Sun, who
an alum from here did,

00:27:18.900 --> 00:27:22.620
did two nice experiments
using the binomial code,

00:27:22.620 --> 00:27:23.620
which Liang Jang

00:27:25.463 --> 00:27:27.420
and I threw together as a summer project

00:27:27.420 --> 00:27:30.220
with an undergraduate Myers Michael

00:27:31.710 --> 00:27:35.170
and got just above breakeven

00:27:36.120 --> 00:27:39.220
in the correct conservatively defined way

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:42.030
in 2023.

00:27:46.650 --> 00:27:51.030
And in the same issue
of nature of Vlad Sivak

00:27:51.030 --> 00:27:56.030
and Michelle's group did a
quantum air correction using

00:27:56.100 --> 00:27:59.710
these beautiful grid states, GKP

00:28:00.630 --> 00:28:05.280
code words and there was an improved

00:28:05.280 --> 00:28:10.280
tantalum transmon, a
reinforcement learning agent

00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:12.240
that helped improve this.

00:28:12.240 --> 00:28:17.040
And on the theory side, a nice

00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:20.220
new semi-autonomous stabilization protocol

00:28:20.220 --> 00:28:24.510
that avoided the measurement
and feedback scheme

00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:28.020
and all of those things
combined to get us up

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:30.063
to an error correction gain of 2.3.

00:28:31.560 --> 00:28:36.270
And really impressively
you could do 400 cycles

00:28:36.270 --> 00:28:37.960
of quantum error correction

00:28:38.925 --> 00:28:41.400
before losing the information.

00:28:41.400 --> 00:28:44.343
So that's really in a class by itself.

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:48.750
Okay, so now I want to turn to the future

00:28:48.750 --> 00:28:50.850
and tell you about a few things

00:28:50.850 --> 00:28:53.820
that are in this monster
paper that I've been saying

00:28:53.820 --> 00:28:55.983
for some time is almost ready.

00:28:57.030 --> 00:29:00.930
It's 12 authors, 137 pages, 533 references

00:29:00.930 --> 00:29:03.000
and we're still working on it

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:06.870
and it's to try to formalize
in a computer science sense,

00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:09.660
some of the instruction set architectures

00:29:09.660 --> 00:29:14.104
that experimentalists
now are using to control

00:29:14.104 --> 00:29:19.080
hybrid systems consisting
of discrete variable qubits

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:21.990
and continuous variable oscillators.

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:25.560
And this is primarily a C2QA project,

00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:29.913
but the Yale part is
also supported by ARO.

00:29:31.230 --> 00:29:34.680
So imagine just as a,
you know, a theorist's

00:29:34.680 --> 00:29:36.960
hardware architecture that you had a two

00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:41.580
or maybe three dimensional
grid of harmonic oscillators

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:44.790
connected by beam splitters,

00:29:44.790 --> 00:29:47.100
which unlike optical beam splitters,

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:50.400
they're microwave activated
using three or four wave mixing.

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:53.430
You can turn them on and off
and you can adjust their rate

00:29:53.430 --> 00:29:55.113
and their phase to,

00:29:56.034 --> 00:29:57.990
to move photons from one box to the other.

00:29:57.990 --> 00:30:02.643
And each box has a single qubit
dispersively coupled to it.

00:30:04.110 --> 00:30:07.740
And let's compare that to an ion trap.

00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:10.350
We're gonna have gates that are similar

00:30:10.350 --> 00:30:13.020
to ion traps, spin dependent forces,

00:30:13.020 --> 00:30:16.830
but in an ion trap each of
the spins is coupled to all

00:30:16.830 --> 00:30:19.890
of the modes and they like to advertise.

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:23.970
This gives them all to all
coupling, but it's complicated

00:30:23.970 --> 00:30:25.950
and you have to, you can get crosstalk

00:30:25.950 --> 00:30:29.310
and there are various complications

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:32.290
although, you know, still
works extremely well

00:30:33.180 --> 00:30:37.050
but there's no controlled
coupling between these modes.

00:30:37.050 --> 00:30:39.930
Whereas here you can be talking

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:42.990
to a single mode very
cleanly with no cross talk

00:30:42.990 --> 00:30:46.380
and then swap that mode
through here to somewhere else

00:30:46.380 --> 00:30:48.090
and have it talk to another qubit

00:30:48.090 --> 00:30:50.910
and come back so that you can communicate

00:30:50.910 --> 00:30:55.910
and entangle the qubits or the cavities.

00:30:56.550 --> 00:31:01.470
So there are sort of three
possible abstract machine models

00:31:01.470 --> 00:31:04.860
that this hardware could
present to the software.

00:31:04.860 --> 00:31:09.210
It could be qubit centric with the

00:31:09.210 --> 00:31:12.330
boons just playing the role
of a communication bust

00:31:12.330 --> 00:31:14.501
that's highly controllable

00:31:14.501 --> 00:31:15.750
and you could have swapping going this way

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:18.210
and swapping going that way
at the same time, as long

00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:21.240
as you schedule the swaps
to avoid collisions.

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:24.540
It could be bosonic mode
centric where the only purpose

00:31:24.540 --> 00:31:25.830
of the qubits is

00:31:25.830 --> 00:31:29.370
to give you universal
control over the oscillators,

00:31:29.370 --> 00:31:32.850
which in turn contain perhaps
quantum error correction

00:31:32.850 --> 00:31:37.850
bosonic codes and it could be hybrid

00:31:38.340 --> 00:31:41.850
in which you have access
at the software level

00:31:41.850 --> 00:31:43.800
to both the discreet variable

00:31:43.800 --> 00:31:46.020
and continuous variable degrees of freedom

00:31:46.020 --> 00:31:49.230
that could be very good for
quantum simulators for spins

00:31:49.230 --> 00:31:51.240
or phormeons coupled to bosons

00:31:51.240 --> 00:31:53.520
such as lattice gauge theories,

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:57.600
which is one of the things
that we're working on in C2QA

00:31:57.600 --> 00:32:00.760
and some particular version of this

00:32:03.030 --> 00:32:06.273
in the dual rail encoding
you'll hear about on Friday.

00:32:07.710 --> 00:32:12.540
So in order to program this thing,

00:32:12.540 --> 00:32:14.460
you have to produce unitaries.

00:32:14.460 --> 00:32:16.800
That's what quantum computers do.

00:32:16.800 --> 00:32:21.520
And so here's a short crash course in

00:32:22.380 --> 00:32:25.380
control theory for these hybrid systems.

00:32:25.380 --> 00:32:29.010
So by driving a two level system

00:32:29.010 --> 00:32:32.490
with classical drives, can you control it?

00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:36.610
Well in some rotating wave
approximation you get control

00:32:37.662 --> 00:32:41.010
of the poly X and the poly Y operators

00:32:41.010 --> 00:32:43.500
and there's a fundamental
theorem from control theory

00:32:43.500 --> 00:32:47.130
that if the lee algebra of
the available controls defined

00:32:47.130 --> 00:32:51.420
by their commutator spans
the space of all operators,

00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:54.040
you have controllability

00:32:56.088 --> 00:32:59.130
and fortunately the commutator

00:32:59.130 --> 00:33:01.740
of the Pauli X and Y gives Pauli Z and

00:33:01.740 --> 00:33:04.020
therefore you have able

00:33:04.020 --> 00:33:08.460
to generate arbitrary SU2
lead group rotations on this

00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:11.553
single qubit using only
two classical controls.

00:33:13.170 --> 00:33:16.740
The same is not true for
a harmonic oscillator.

00:33:16.740 --> 00:33:19.920
Your two classical drives, cosine

00:33:19.920 --> 00:33:24.570
and sine couple you in the
sum rotating frame to position

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:27.030
and momentum and
unfortunately the commutator

00:33:27.030 --> 00:33:30.000
of those guys is just a number and

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:32.010
therefore the algebra closes

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:34.950
and you cannot do anything other than

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:38.580
displace the oscillator in phase space.

00:33:38.580 --> 00:33:42.270
You can make coherent states
but you can't make fox states

00:33:42.270 --> 00:33:45.393
or GKP states or binomial code words.

00:33:46.975 --> 00:33:50.580
And we know also physically
that's closely related

00:33:50.580 --> 00:33:52.893
to the fact that the
level spacing is uniform.

00:33:53.850 --> 00:33:57.570
But in this dispersive
coupling hamiltonian

00:33:57.570 --> 00:34:00.930
where you have a qubit
coupled to the cavity

00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:04.620
and their frequencies are
different than you get this

00:34:04.620 --> 00:34:06.390
dispersive coupling

00:34:06.390 --> 00:34:09.870
and by applying drives to the cavity

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:12.220
and the qubit, you can

00:34:13.110 --> 00:34:15.300
reduce this nonlinearity

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:19.470
to something slightly
less nonlinear to just X

00:34:19.470 --> 00:34:22.293
and P times one of the Pauli matrices.

00:34:23.400 --> 00:34:26.850
So if you look at the
commutator algebra for

00:34:26.850 --> 00:34:29.910
that set of controls for example X

00:34:29.910 --> 00:34:32.610
the oscillator position times Pauli X,

00:34:32.610 --> 00:34:34.440
momentum times Pauli Y,

00:34:34.440 --> 00:34:36.300
you get something interesting

00:34:36.300 --> 00:34:38.400
because the Pauli matrix is anti-qubit

00:34:38.400 --> 00:34:42.000
you get conditional squeezing hamiltonian,

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:45.670
it will squeeze the
position if Z is plus one

00:34:46.951 --> 00:34:49.113
and squeeze the momentum
if Z is minus one.

00:34:51.300 --> 00:34:53.700
If you take further commutators, you see

00:34:53.700 --> 00:34:57.030
that the polynomial
grows and grows in degree

00:34:57.030 --> 00:35:00.060
and the operator algebra does not close

00:35:00.060 --> 00:35:02.250
and you get universal control.

00:35:02.250 --> 00:35:05.130
So then knowing that
there are standard tricks,

00:35:05.130 --> 00:35:08.100
Trotter-Suzuki to add control terms

00:35:08.100 --> 00:35:12.570
and Baker Campbell Hausdorf,
to multiply them in the form

00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:15.690
of commutators and you can then

00:35:15.690 --> 00:35:19.680
generate the most general Hamiltonian

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:23.340
that a single qubit coupled to
a single oscillator can have,

00:35:23.340 --> 00:35:27.270
namely some polynomial of
X and P for the oscillator

00:35:27.270 --> 00:35:30.270
and a vector of three
polynomials dotted into

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:31.500
the Pauli system.

00:35:31.500 --> 00:35:34.713
And that gives you universal control.

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:41.010
So there are many instruction sets

00:35:41.010 --> 00:35:42.540
that we consider in this paper,

00:35:42.540 --> 00:35:44.580
but I'll stick to one example here,

00:35:44.580 --> 00:35:46.710
which is the control displacement,

00:35:46.710 --> 00:35:51.240
which is generated exponentials
of that control set

00:35:51.240 --> 00:35:54.970
and plus single qubit
rotations around axes

00:35:56.070 --> 00:35:59.400
on the equator of the block sphere.

00:35:59.400 --> 00:36:02.670
And by alternating these,
you're doing a kind

00:36:02.670 --> 00:36:07.070
of quantum signal processing extended to

00:36:10.905 --> 00:36:13.590
where the rotation angles on the

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:16.710
qubit are now quantum objects themselves

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:19.140
that don't necessarily
commute the position

00:36:19.140 --> 00:36:20.690
and momentum of the oscillator.

00:36:21.710 --> 00:36:23.580
And so here are some examples from

00:36:23.580 --> 00:36:27.630
Alec Eickbusch's paper in
the from the Devoret group.

00:36:27.630 --> 00:36:29.760
This is the characteristic function for

00:36:29.760 --> 00:36:32.940
what is obviously a highly squeezed state.

00:36:32.940 --> 00:36:36.480
Here is the same for
the binomial code words.

00:36:36.480 --> 00:36:40.020
This is all data and this
is the GKP code words

00:36:40.020 --> 00:36:44.100
and these can be made in
very shallow circuits.

00:36:44.100 --> 00:36:47.460
This is a very expressive gate set in the

00:36:47.460 --> 00:36:49.113
machine learning language.

00:36:50.059 --> 00:36:51.900
So what can we do with these?

00:36:51.900 --> 00:36:54.780
Well the first thing is
you can do the world's most

00:36:54.780 --> 00:36:59.650
complicated single qubit rotation by

00:37:01.650 --> 00:37:05.550
doing a series of controlled

00:37:05.550 --> 00:37:08.700
and uncontrolled
displacement in such a way

00:37:08.700 --> 00:37:12.720
by applying these spin dependent
forces to the oscillator.

00:37:12.720 --> 00:37:14.190
If the qubit is in one,

00:37:14.190 --> 00:37:17.580
you make a counterclockwise
circuit in phase space

00:37:17.580 --> 00:37:19.560
and pick up one berry phase.

00:37:19.560 --> 00:37:22.110
And if qubit is zero,
you pick up the opposite.

00:37:22.110 --> 00:37:24.090
And the net effect of that is no matter

00:37:24.090 --> 00:37:28.230
what was in the cavity before
it's the same, unchanged.

00:37:28.230 --> 00:37:32.250
But the qubit gets a phase
kickback, which rotates it

00:37:32.250 --> 00:37:33.240
around the Z axis.

00:37:33.240 --> 00:37:36.210
And here you can see the
Ramsay fringe oscillations

00:37:36.210 --> 00:37:39.240
measuring that rotation.

00:37:39.240 --> 00:37:41.430
So can we extend that

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:44.250
to do something interesting
within this qubit

00:37:44.250 --> 00:37:46.290
centric abstract machine model

00:37:46.290 --> 00:37:48.150
where the cavities do something

00:37:48.150 --> 00:37:51.000
to the qubit, not the other way around.

00:37:51.000 --> 00:37:55.680
And Kevin Smith has developed a series

00:37:55.680 --> 00:37:58.620
of entangling gates, so the RZZ theta,

00:37:58.620 --> 00:38:01.980
this two qubit rotation
you can achieve let's say

00:38:01.980 --> 00:38:03.400
between this qubit

00:38:03.400 --> 00:38:07.860
and that qubit by doing a
control displacement in the first

00:38:07.860 --> 00:38:11.070
cavity, then moving the contents
of that cavity down here,

00:38:11.070 --> 00:38:13.050
doing a different
control displacement with

00:38:13.050 --> 00:38:16.740
that guy, moving it back
and forth a couple of times.

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:18.900
And you can get one

00:38:18.900 --> 00:38:22.170
of four trajectories
depending on the four values

00:38:22.170 --> 00:38:23.787
of Z1 and Z2.

00:38:23.787 --> 00:38:25.098
And if Z1

00:38:25.098 --> 00:38:27.933
and Z2 are the same,

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:33.270
you make a clockwise loop either here

00:38:33.270 --> 00:38:35.910
or here depending on whether
it's zero, zero or one one.

00:38:35.910 --> 00:38:37.560
And if they're the opposite,

00:38:37.560 --> 00:38:40.050
you go counterclockwise either here

00:38:40.050 --> 00:38:42.360
or here picking up opposite phase and

00:38:42.360 --> 00:38:44.193
therefore doing this gate.

00:38:45.060 --> 00:38:46.800
Now there are simple...

00:38:46.800 --> 00:38:49.710
you could have bigger swap networks so

00:38:49.710 --> 00:38:53.412
that you entangle this qubit
with that qubit by swapping

00:38:53.412 --> 00:38:56.250
through some path this way.

00:38:56.250 --> 00:38:58.320
And unlike Malmer-Sorenson

00:38:58.320 --> 00:39:00.630
and ion traps, you don't
do this to everybody.

00:39:00.630 --> 00:39:02.850
You select who you do this to

00:39:02.850 --> 00:39:05.133
and you can do several
of them in parallel.

00:39:06.780 --> 00:39:10.600
Kevin's also found similar
circuits that will produce CNOT

00:39:10.600 --> 00:39:15.600
ZZZ of theta, Toffoli gates, et cetera.

00:39:15.810 --> 00:39:20.700
So that's a nice example
of using the cavities

00:39:20.700 --> 00:39:25.700
as communication bus plus
things that can entangle

00:39:26.162 --> 00:39:29.670
the qubits in a qubit centric model.

00:39:29.670 --> 00:39:33.990
In a boson centric model, we
want to use the same gate set

00:39:33.990 --> 00:39:38.610
to do things to the
oscillators using the qubits

00:39:38.610 --> 00:39:39.780
with the same gate set.

00:39:39.780 --> 00:39:44.550
And one example is an improved circuit

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:47.160
for GKP bosonic code

00:39:47.160 --> 00:39:49.860
stabilization and gates.

00:39:49.860 --> 00:39:52.830
And this is what was used
in the first part of this is

00:39:52.830 --> 00:39:55.770
what was used in the recent experiment

00:39:55.770 --> 00:39:57.630
from Michelle's group.

00:39:57.630 --> 00:39:58.650
So I'll just remind you

00:39:58.650 --> 00:40:02.440
that the idealized GKP wave functions

00:40:03.430 --> 00:40:06.240
are a picket fence,
evenly spaced in position,

00:40:06.240 --> 00:40:09.450
and it's where a transform
is a picket fence easily

00:40:09.450 --> 00:40:11.553
evenly spaced in momentum.

00:40:12.390 --> 00:40:17.390
And these are defined by
stabilizers, two stabilizers,

00:40:17.850 --> 00:40:21.060
which are a momentum boost

00:40:21.060 --> 00:40:24.030
and a position displacement.

00:40:24.030 --> 00:40:26.310
You can see, understand
the displace, you know,

00:40:26.310 --> 00:40:28.320
if you displace this
by a lattice constant,

00:40:28.320 --> 00:40:29.520
it comes back to itself.

00:40:29.520 --> 00:40:32.460
So it's a plus one eigenstate of that.

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:36.976
And so the stabilizers in the code

00:40:36.976 --> 00:40:39.210
are translations in phase space.

00:40:39.210 --> 00:40:41.910
How do we measure the eigen
value of those stabilizers?

00:40:41.910 --> 00:40:44.580
Well, unlike the Pauli
stabilizers you're used to,

00:40:44.580 --> 00:40:46.680
they're not plus one or minus one.

00:40:46.680 --> 00:40:49.530
They have a phase that
lives on the unit circle

00:40:49.530 --> 00:40:51.120
and they're sensitive.

00:40:51.120 --> 00:40:54.189
That phase is sensitive to
small displacement errors

00:40:54.189 --> 00:40:57.120
of this lattice of states.

00:40:57.120 --> 00:41:00.420
And the way you measure the
eigen value associated with

00:41:00.420 --> 00:41:05.420
that unitary is with a phase
kickback circuit like this

00:41:05.910 --> 00:41:07.240
much as was used

00:41:09.632 --> 00:41:11.700
in that Ramsay fringe
experiment I showed you.

00:41:11.700 --> 00:41:16.470
So you can reinterpret

00:41:16.470 --> 00:41:21.450
this momentum boost when you put a Z there

00:41:21.450 --> 00:41:23.010
instead of a momentum boost.

00:41:23.010 --> 00:41:25.770
That depends on whether Z
is plus one or minus one.

00:41:25.770 --> 00:41:29.100
You can think of it as
a rotation of the qubit

00:41:29.100 --> 00:41:30.570
around the Z axis.

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:33.180
That depends on the
position of the oscillator.

00:41:33.180 --> 00:41:35.490
And if the positions are on a lattice

00:41:35.490 --> 00:41:38.850
and they're in the right place,
that rotation is a multiple

00:41:38.850 --> 00:41:42.240
of two pi and doesn't give
you any change in the state

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:43.733
of the qubit.

00:41:44.940 --> 00:41:47.703
But if there's an error,
you pick up some signal,

00:41:49.020 --> 00:41:51.060
well that's fine for the ideal world,

00:41:51.060 --> 00:41:54.060
but in practice you have
to have a Gaussian envelope

00:41:54.060 --> 00:41:56.640
and finite squeezing
of these picket fences.

00:41:56.640 --> 00:42:00.630
And so there's some quantum
uncertainty in the rotation

00:42:00.630 --> 00:42:03.030
angle of the qubit.

00:42:03.030 --> 00:42:06.570
And it's not always
exactly two pi multiple

00:42:06.570 --> 00:42:09.750
of two pi, even when
there's no actual error

00:42:09.750 --> 00:42:12.330
because of this quantum uncertainty.

00:42:12.330 --> 00:42:13.713
So that's a problem.

00:42:14.853 --> 00:42:19.050
And also, you know, we
have to deal with this fact

00:42:19.050 --> 00:42:21.120
that there's an overall Gaussian envelope.

00:42:21.120 --> 00:42:22.980
So how do we handle this?

00:42:22.980 --> 00:42:26.160
And it's too complicated to explain now,

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:30.150
but there's a wonderful
autonomous protocol rather than

00:42:30.150 --> 00:42:34.636
measuring the small shifts,
you run this circuit developed

00:42:34.636 --> 00:42:39.636
by these folks where you
have the stabilizer in there

00:42:43.230 --> 00:42:46.050
but with a controlled
gate as I showed you.

00:42:46.050 --> 00:42:50.820
And then you pre and post
pendat with very small

00:42:50.820 --> 00:42:53.550
perpendicular displacement in phase space

00:42:53.550 --> 00:42:56.820
which actually turn out if
you choose them correctly,

00:42:56.820 --> 00:42:58.380
to cancel out the effect

00:42:58.380 --> 00:43:01.350
of these Gaussian
fluctuations in position.

00:43:01.350 --> 00:43:03.120
And then rather than measuring

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:07.830
and feedback, you just
reset the qubit or measure

00:43:07.830 --> 00:43:12.490
and reset it and it pumps away the entropy

00:43:14.250 --> 00:43:17.253
from the system without
having to do direct feedback.

00:43:19.590 --> 00:43:23.580
So that was a interesting step forward.

00:43:23.580 --> 00:43:27.150
Now if you want to have the GKP code,

00:43:27.150 --> 00:43:32.070
and it's interesting fact that
the Pauli unitary like the X,

00:43:32.070 --> 00:43:34.860
Y or Z unitaries are just displacement.

00:43:34.860 --> 00:43:36.690
They're very easy to do,

00:43:36.690 --> 00:43:38.310
whether you just send the classical pulse

00:43:38.310 --> 00:43:41.520
and change the position of the oscillator.

00:43:41.520 --> 00:43:45.300
But how in the world could we
do a Pauli rotation, let's say

00:43:45.300 --> 00:43:48.630
around the x axis, which
is a super position

00:43:48.630 --> 00:43:52.803
of not doing a displacement
and doing a displacement.

00:43:54.060 --> 00:43:56.880
Those are sort of macroscopically
distinguishable things.

00:43:56.880 --> 00:43:58.290
How do you do that?

00:43:58.290 --> 00:44:02.100
And it turns out Shada Singh has...

00:44:02.100 --> 00:44:05.610
who's working with me in her degree has

00:44:05.610 --> 00:44:10.610
modified this small big
small stabilization protocol

00:44:10.830 --> 00:44:13.800
that does autonomously,
does the error correction,

00:44:13.800 --> 00:44:17.010
it just splits this middle
step into two pieces

00:44:17.010 --> 00:44:19.740
and inserts a qubit rotation in between.

00:44:19.740 --> 00:44:23.880
So it actually takes almost
exactly the same length

00:44:23.880 --> 00:44:26.250
of time as the stabilization process

00:44:26.250 --> 00:44:28.770
and the stabilization keeps working.

00:44:28.770 --> 00:44:31.920
And so it protects against
photon loss in the cavity

00:44:31.920 --> 00:44:33.780
or small phase based displacement.

00:44:33.780 --> 00:44:36.630
And it also has some favorable
properties with respect

00:44:36.630 --> 00:44:38.580
to the ancilla dephasing errors.

00:44:38.580 --> 00:44:41.100
And this looks a little
bit like ordinary gate

00:44:41.100 --> 00:44:43.470
teleportation, but actually it's not

00:44:43.470 --> 00:44:44.730
because this second half

00:44:44.730 --> 00:44:47.673
of the circuit is not the
inverse of the first half.

00:44:48.600 --> 00:44:53.523
And so that's something
that's being written up now.

00:44:55.200 --> 00:44:58.717
So there are many open questions.

00:44:58.717 --> 00:45:01.860
What's the analog of
randomized benchmarking

00:45:01.860 --> 00:45:04.059
for continuous variable systems?

00:45:04.059 --> 00:45:07.170
there are analogs between
Gaussian operations

00:45:07.170 --> 00:45:09.870
and Clifford operations on qubits,

00:45:09.870 --> 00:45:13.260
but the Gaussian
operations are non-compact.

00:45:13.260 --> 00:45:16.957
They involve Bogoliubov
transformations, not SU2,

00:45:18.900 --> 00:45:21.423
they don't form a two
design for the theorists.

00:45:24.120 --> 00:45:26.550
Learning how to do this non abelian

00:45:26.550 --> 00:45:28.800
quantum signal processing using both

00:45:28.800 --> 00:45:31.650
momentum boosts and position displacement

00:45:31.650 --> 00:45:34.200
as rotation angles on the qubits.

00:45:34.200 --> 00:45:36.030
But is there a formal theory

00:45:36.030 --> 00:45:39.090
to give us convergence bounds
on how well that works?

00:45:39.090 --> 00:45:41.988
And is there a nice way to construct

00:45:41.988 --> 00:45:45.840
useful unitaries other than
numerical optimization?

00:45:45.840 --> 00:45:47.640
And we've made some progress on that,

00:45:47.640 --> 00:45:49.833
but still lots more work needs to be done.

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:55.240
So this is a very incomplete history

00:45:56.130 --> 00:45:59.160
and I just want to say thank
you to the many, many people,

00:45:59.160 --> 00:46:03.300
all of whom I can't list here

00:46:03.300 --> 00:46:06.420
that I've had the pleasure
of working with recently

00:46:06.420 --> 00:46:10.860
inside YQI from postdocs, students,

00:46:10.860 --> 00:46:14.340
undergrads, and even
a high school student.

00:46:14.340 --> 00:46:18.600
And many thanks to my faculty colleagues

00:46:18.600 --> 00:46:20.160
with whom I am working

00:46:20.160 --> 00:46:23.550
and to a few more that we're excited

00:46:23.550 --> 00:46:25.620
to be recruiting right now.

00:46:25.620 --> 00:46:30.190
And finally, it happens
that this month is a

00:46:31.380 --> 00:46:35.953
palindromic birthday for Rob
when expressed in binary.

00:46:35.953 --> 00:46:37.970
(audience laughing)

00:46:37.970 --> 00:46:41.550
And so I want to wish Rob a happy birthday

00:46:41.550 --> 00:46:43.440
and thank him for so many years

00:46:43.440 --> 00:46:45.603
of just fantastic collaboration.

00:46:46.783 --> 00:46:49.783
(audience clapping)

00:47:02.102 --> 00:47:05.352
(light ambient music)

